    isbefore: []  # 58c8
    duplicate_of: []  # 58c8
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 58c8
    pr_ref:  # 58c8
    description: "The GitHub-arm test harness stubs the network by PREPENDING to PATH, so any\ntool it did not think to fake falls through to the real binary -- and one of\nthem reaches the live GitHub API.\n\nSYMPTOM (reproduced 2026-07-30 on clean origin/dev in an isolated worktree, so\nthis predates and is unrelated to WI-bisar):\n\n  tests/test_contribute_github.py::TestGitHubFallbackWhenGhAbsent\n      ::test_github_without_gh_falls_back_to_api_post   FAILED\n\n  E   HTTP 401: Bad credentials (https://api.github.com/graphql)\n  E   Try authenticating with:  gh auth login\n\nMECHANISM\n\n  tests/_forge_github_harness.py:205\n      env[\"PATH\"] = f\"{bindir}:{env['PATH']}\"          <- prepend, not replace\n\n  tests/_forge_github_harness.py:179\n      def bindir_with_fakes(tmp_path, *, gh: bool = False)\n      \"\"\"A PATH-prependable dir holding the fake curl (+ optional gh).\"\"\"\n\nWith `gh=False` the harness places no fake `gh` in bindir -- but the real one\nis still further down PATH (`/usr/bin/gh` on this machine), so `command -v gh`\nsucceeds and the script under test takes its gh branch. The class is literally\nnamed `TestGitHubFallbackWhenGhAbsent`; gh is not absent, and nothing enforces\nthat it is.\n\nTWO DEFECTS, NOT ONE\n\n1. The test's premise is unenforced and environment-dependent. It passes where\n   gh happens not to be installed (CI self-hosted runners today) and fails\n   where it is. Its green in CI is an accident of the runner image, not\n   evidence that the fallback path works -- a passing test measuring nothing.\n   19 call sites across 6 files use `bindir_with_fakes(...)` without `gh=True`\n   and therefore share the premise:\n     test_autopr_github_arm.py, test_verify_tracker_pr_github.py,\n     test_ci_debug_github.py, test_contribute_github.py,\n     test_list_my_prs_github.py, test_resolve_forge_token_github.py\n\n2. A unit test makes a live network call. The harness docstring says \"The\n   network boundary is stubbed two ways, both placed on PATH\", which is exactly\n   the leak: a PATH-PREPEND stub only intercepts the tools you remembered to\n   fake. api.github.com is allowlisted for forge API calls so this is not a\n   policy violation, but an unattended test suite reaching the real API -- with\n   whatever ambient credentials the machine has -- is not something to leave in\n   place. On a machine with valid gh auth this would not 401; it would create a\n   real pull request against the fixture's `up/r` upstream.\n\nCANDIDATE FIXES\n\n  (a) Replace PATH instead of prepending: build a curated bindir containing\n      symlinks to the tools the scripts legitimately need (git, bash, sed,\n      ...) and nothing else, so an unfaked tool is genuinely unresolvable\n      rather than silently real. Directly fixes the class.\n  (b) Add a `gh` shim that simulates absence. Does NOT work as stated -- the\n      scripts probe with `command -v gh`, which a shim satisfies. It would have\n      to exit 127 AND the probe would have to check exit status, so this\n      depends on the callers' probe style; verify before choosing.\n  (c) Assert the premise in the harness: when `gh=False`, fail loudly at setup\n      if `gh` resolves on the constructed PATH. Cheap, and converts a silent\n      environment-dependent pass into a loud one. Composes with (a) as the\n      guard that keeps it fixed.\n\n(a) + (c) is the structural pair: (a) closes the leak, (c) makes a reopening\nfail at setup instead of producing a vacuous green.\n\nGENERALIZABLE FORM (candidate durable lesson): a PATH-prepend sandbox is an\nallowlist implemented as a blocklist -- it intercepts what you enumerated and\npasses through everything else. The failure is silent by construction, because\nthe un-faked tool works."  # 58c8
    fields: {}  # 58c8
